G7 run 2, 2026-09-05. Crossings declared per amendment 2. Depth AND Hamming.
Hamming discharged by injecting the property into the module that owns the
crossing net, then running SymbiYosys BMC to depth 14.

================= CDC-1 / gold   crossing=flag_src =================
VERDICT        SRC         DST         WIDTH DEPTH  SOURCE
SAFE           (declared)  clk_dst         1     2  flag_src
crossings: 1   SAFE=1                                            exit 0

================= CDC-1 / gate   crossing=flag_src =================
VERDICT        SRC         DST         WIDTH DEPTH  SOURCE
DEPTH_1        (declared)  clk_dst         1     1  flag_src
crossings: 1   DEPTH_1=1                                         exit 1

================= CDC-2 / gold   crossing=gray =================
VERDICT        SRC         DST         WIDTH DEPTH  SOURCE
MULTIBIT       (declared)  clk             4     2  gray
hamming gray   PROVEN     no counterexample to depth 14
crossings: 1   SAFE=1                                            exit 0

================= CDC-2 / gate   crossing=bin =================
VERDICT        SRC         DST         WIDTH DEPTH  SOURCE
MULTIBIT       (declared)  clk             4     2  bin
hamming bin    REFUTED    counterexample
crossings: 1   MULTIBIT_UNSAFE=1                                 exit 1


THE COUNTEREXAMPLE, parsed from ham_bin/ham/engine_0/trace.vcd
--------------------------------------------------------------
step  bin   prev  diff  popcount  rst_n  boot
   0  0000  0000  0000      0     ?     0000
   1  0000  0000  0000      0     0     0000
   2  0000  0000  0000      0     0     0001
   3  0000  0000  0000      0     1     0010
   4  0001  0000  0001      1     1     0011
   5  0010  0001  0011      2     1     0100   <-- ASSERT FAILS HERE

The crossing bus goes 0001 to 0010 and TWO bits change in one cycle. A
receiver in another clock domain sampling mid-transition can latch 0000 or
0011, neither of which is the old value or the new one. That is the entire
reason gray coding exists, and CDC-2's gate.v moved the encoder to the far
side of the synchronizer so the bus that physically crosses is raw binary.

The boot counter behaves as intended and is visible in the trace: rst_n is
held low at steps 1 and 2, released from step 3 onward, and the assertion
arms at boot >= 4, which is step 5.


SCORING
-------
C1  CONFIRMED.  Depth check flags CDC-1/gate.v as DEPTH_1 and passes gold.v.
C2  CONFIRMED.  Hamming refutes CDC-2/gate.v with a counterexample in which
    two consecutive values differ in more than one bit, and proves gold.v.

Both cases are ones REPORT section 7.4 records every functional checker
getting wrong or being unable to express. cec and dsec cannot build a miter
for CDC-1 at all; every checker correctly calls CDC-2 equivalent, because it
is. The defect survives all of them and G7 names it in both directions.


THREE TOOL BUGS FOUND ON THE WAY, ALL MINE
------------------------------------------
1. A wrapper module reaching into the DUT as dut.<net> does not work: Yosys
   treats the hierarchical reference as an implicitly declared wire, and a
   `.*` port connection needs every port redeclared in the wrapper. Replaced
   by instrumenting the source, which is what commercial CDC tools do anyway.
   The instrumented file is written into the work directory so the property
   that was actually checked can be read rather than trusted.

2. The module-name regex ended in a literal backspace byte, 0x08, instead of
   the two characters backslash-b, so it matched nothing and every Hamming
   check returned "could not find module to instrument". Written by a shell
   heredoc that interpreted the escape.

3. THE ONE WORTH RECORDING. The first working Hamming run refuted BOTH gold
   and gate at step 0. A BMC engine starts from an ARBITRARY state, so
   cdc_prev_r began as garbage and the first comparison refuted every design
   including correct ones. This is the same trap experiments/fsm_reencode hit
   with PDR a month earlier, and it took the same fix: an initialised boot
   counter that holds reset asserted for two cycles, releases it for the rest
   of the trace, and arms the assertion only after the design settles. A
   checker that refutes everything looks exactly like a checker that works,
   until you run it on something you know is correct. Running gold and gate
   through every check, rather than only the case expected to fail, is what
   caught it.
